  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "The set $A$ and its boundary $\u2202A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36},\\,\u2202A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/f3b1df5f948603edcc1095da9215145cba21c7aebbdfdd6ab5cacecb5810efc7.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/7bc1b41e3ca8aba5c1a5658ec421f7358041c57dea7c1ef45fe265c62664f097.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ satisfy the equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\u2202A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'.",
